WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.329
Episode fourteen: write a plugin, a performance metric.

2
00:00:04.329 --> 00:00:10.233
This video assumes APEX installed as the README describes, and Python 3.12.

3
00:00:10.233 --> 00:00:14.507
That is episode one.

4
00:00:14.507 --> 00:00:26.779
Almost everything in APEX is a plugin: methods, update strategies, localizations, priors, simulators, executors, metrics, optimizers, objectives and figures.

5
00:00:26.779 --> 00:00:28.733
Each kind has a registry.

6
00:00:28.733 --> 00:00:48.057
And uv run apex plugins lists every registry and its plugins.

7
00:00:48.057 --> 00:00:51.544
Create a package of your own, outside the APEX folder.

8
00:00:51.544 --> 00:01:16.390
Its one file is a metric: the mean absolute data error of each member, in units of the observation errors.

9
00:01:16.390 --> 00:01:17.762
What APEX takes from it.

10
00:01:17.762 --> 00:01:22.796
The options are the constructor's keyword arguments, with their type hints and defaults.

11
00:01:22.796 --> 00:01:23.482
Here, scale.

12
00:01:23.482 --> 00:01:37.214
The summary is the first line of the class docstring.

13
00:01:37.214 --> 00:01:39.781
Evaluate returns one value per member.

14
00:01:39.781 --> 00:01:47.552
Arrays keep the ensemble on the last axis: the forecast is (n_data, n_members), a state variable (size, n_members).

15
00:01:47.552 --> 00:02:04.806
And raise ValueError with a clear message for invalid options or inputs, as the constructor does.

16
00:02:04.806 --> 00:02:09.717
In your pyproject.toml, add an entry point in the registry's group.

17
00:02:09.717 --> 00:02:26.721
Its name is the plugin's name in configurations: here, mae, in the group apex.metrics.

18
00:02:26.721 --> 00:02:30.350
Install your package into the environment that runs APEX.

19
00:02:30.350 --> 00:02:41.315
We do it from the APEX folder.

20
00:02:41.315 --> 00:02:43.176
We check that APEX finds it.

21
00:02:43.176 --> 00:02:56.226
APEX finds entry points when a registry is first used, so nothing else is needed.

22
00:02:56.226 --> 00:02:57.604
One thing to know.

23
00:02:57.604 --> 00:03:04.953
A later plain uv sync makes the environment exactly APEX's lock again, and removes your package.

24
00:03:04.953 --> 00:03:16.803
Install it again, or use uv sync --locked --inexact, which keeps it.

25
00:03:16.803 --> 00:03:24.973
Next: Copy examples/linear_gaussian/config_esmda.toml to config_mae.toml in the same folder, and give its run a name of its own.

26
00:03:24.973 --> 00:03:26.888
We did that before this scene.

27
00:03:26.888 --> 00:03:28.165
Then add the metric.

28
00:03:28.165 --> 00:03:44.035
Listing [[metrics]] replaces the default metrics, so name the ones you also want.

29
00:03:44.035 --> 00:03:53.382
We go to the linear-Gaussian example's folder.

30
00:03:53.382 --> 00:03:55.333
We validate the configuration.

31
00:03:55.333 --> 00:04:10.909
APEX refuses unknown or mistyped options when a configuration is validated.

32
00:04:10.909 --> 00:04:22.872
Then we run it.

33
00:04:22.872 --> 00:04:27.120
The summary now has a column for it, from the prior to the result.

34
00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:49.739
The same values are in the run's checkpoints, in apex report --plots, on the web UI's run page and available to the agents.

35
00:04:49.739 --> 00:04:53.016
Finally, a test against a value you can work out by hand.

36
00:04:53.016 --> 00:05:08.534
It also finds the metric through its entry point.

37
00:05:08.534 --> 00:05:20.108
We run the test.

38
00:05:20.108 --> 00:05:25.608
Thank you for watching.
